1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:06,000
What's worth replicating — and has anyone checked it?

2
00:00:07,000 --> 00:00:18,000
Ranked off the OpenAIRE Graph: impact, momentum,
and whether the materials to re-run it exist.

3
00:00:19,000 --> 00:00:24,000
It overlays what the Graph can't:
which claims were independently checked.

4
00:00:25,000 --> 00:00:34,000
Open one — the signed Science Live nanopublication:
the outcome and its evidence.

5
00:00:35,000 --> 00:00:44,000
High-impact, but no linked materials —
so it can't be re-run.

6
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:54,000
This one's reproducible: code and data linked,
archived in Software Heritage.

7
00:00:54,000 --> 00:01:00,000
A live FAIR-software read, plus RSE practices —
documented, tested, CI.

8
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:05,000
Hit Replicate: the FORRT template —
step one is the replication-radar MCP.

9
00:01:06,000 --> 00:01:14,000
So we switch to the agent —
the same engine, now an MCP.

10
00:01:18,000 --> 00:01:32,000
The agent asks the Radar what's un-replicated:
Oliver 2018 — 1,700 citations, never checked.

11
00:01:38,000 --> 00:01:52,000
It finds independent software — XMHW —
and catches one that only looks independent.

12
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:14,000
It hands off to the OpenAIRE MCP for independent
data — a true replication, not a re-run.

13
00:02:20,000 --> 00:02:32,000
Honest scope: the satellite era only;
the century-long trend stays open.

14
00:02:34,000 --> 00:02:44,000
It creates the repo and records
the paper, data, and software.

15
00:02:45,000 --> 00:02:49,500
Two MCPs on one Graph — discover, then check.
Replication Radar.
